 #:seon.task{:name "a-reference-with-no-path-is-a-missing-file"
             :plan "`seon.ref/section` of a reference with no path (\"#the-goal\", \"#\") must answer a `:seon.ref/section-error` with `:seon.ref/section-missing` `:file`, as \"the evidence\" does; today it throws, because it calls `seon.ref/locate` with \"\" and `locate` is contracted `[:cat :seon.ref/path]` (a string of length at least 1). Two changes, both needed, and the second is why two agents (rounds 1 and 2) did not finish: (1) in `seon.ref/section`, call `seon.ref/locate` only for a non-empty path, `(when (seq path) (locate path))`, so an empty path takes the missing-file branch; (2) the missing-file branch builds the error with `seon.ref/section-error`, whose contract `[:cat :keyword :seon.ref/path ...]` and whose output schema `:seon.ref/section-error` both require `:seon.ref/path` (`[:string {:min 1}]`), so an empty path fails them. Fix at the producer: `seon.ref/section-error` takes `[:maybe :seon.ref/path]` and omits the key when the path is empty (`(not-empty path)`, also from `:seon.error/offending`; absence is no key), and the schema `:seon.ref/section-error` makes `:seon.ref/path` optional (register it with `(seon.schema/register! :seon.ref/section-error [:and :seon.error/base [:map [:seon.ref/section-missing :seon.ref/section-missing] [:seon.ref/path {:optional true} :seon.ref/path] [:seon.ref/anchor {:optional true} :seon.ref/anchor] [:seon.ref/nearest :seon.ref/nearest]]])` and keep `:seon.ref/path` itself unchanged, its other users need a non-empty path); `:seon.ref/nearest` for an empty path is already the Markdown files of the checkout root, from `seon.ref/nearest-files`. Verified at an agent's REPL (2026-10-10): with the schema registered and `section-error` and `section` redefined this way, `(seon.ref/section \"#the-goal\")` answers `{:seon.ref/section-missing :file :seon.ref/anchor \"the-goal\"}` and no path. Read a schema with `(doc :seon.ref/section-error)` (a keyword is not a symbol: `my.program/source` refuses it); `seon.schema/snapshot` is not the schema listing. Proofs: `seon.ref-test/a-reference-with-no-path-is-a-missing-file` (three rows, the first already green) and `seon.ref-test/a-reference-with-no-path-answers-an-error-that-holds-no-path` (the error's anchor, no :seon.ref/path key, the evidence among the nearest). Read `my.fs/section` and `README.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "design-a-pathless-reference-section"
             :plan "## Problem\n\n`seon.ref/section` of a reference with no path, such as `\"#the-goal\"`, throws instead of answering a missing-file `:seon.ref/section-error`, as it does for a path that names no file. On main:\n\n```clojure\n(seon.ref/section \"#the-goal\")\n;; throws: seon.ref/locate refused argument 0 `path`: expected :seon.ref/path (a string of length at least 1), got a string.\n```\n"
             :instructions "## Work\n\nMake the intent say exactly what the problem is, in terms of the data. Read `## Problem` first. Then search the system for what already exists: `my.program/search` with the words of the problem, `docs/seon/architecture/vocabulary.md` for the words we use, `my.program/source` on each owner you find. Change the intent with `my.task/refine!`, passing the whole new intent text: `(my.task/refine! {:seon.task/plan whole-new-intent})`. Keep `## Problem` as it is and add:\n\n- `## What exists`: the producer of the wrong or missing value, its callers, the schemas its inputs and outputs use, and the tests that cover it, each cited in backticks as a fully qualified symbol or keyword.\n- `## Approach`: the data first (the schema keys involved, and any schema to add or change, registered on this branch), then each function to change or add with its name, arity and complete contract over named schemas, and what it replaces or deletes. A new function is a `defn` in its owner's namespace with its full contract and the body `(throw (ex-info \"not yet\" {:seon.error/type :seon.error/unimplemented}))`.\n\nBacktick every name you cite: a backticked name that does not resolve shows up as a problem to fix.\n\n## Done\n\nThe three sections have content, every reference resolves, the intent names the schemas involved, and every function you defined or changed has a complete contract. Your proofs show what is missing after each evaluation.\n"}
]
